fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let (X,\tau ) be a topological space and let A\subseteq X. The topology
\tau _A=\{A\cap U\mid U\in \tau \}
on A is the subspace topology, also called the relative topology, induced by X. Thus a subset of A may be open in the subspace even when it is not open as a subset of X.

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
Let \mathcal {U} be an open cover of A by sets open in its subspace topology. For every U\in \mathcal {U}, choose an open subset V_U\subseteq X with U=A\cap V_U. Then
\{X\setminus A\}\cup \{V_U\mid U\in \mathcal {U}\}
is an open cover of X. A finite subcover of X, after discarding X\setminus A, yields a finite subcover of A by members of \mathcal {U}.

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Hausdorffness does not force compactness. On the real line, the collection
\{(-n,n)\mid n\in \mathbb {N},\;n\geq 1\}
is an open cover of \mathbb {R}, but every finite subcollection is contained in one interval (-N,N) and therefore fails to cover the real line.
